#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173097} :activity/id #uuid "4e3e11c5-f10e-59a8-a6b4-34e2c5e85cc6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050142 :topic/id #uuid "30510c62-b4f5-5b4d-9d00-f64786d1c9a9" :topic/math-academy-id 3546} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173174 :tutorial/content "Recall that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the parabola's vertex.\n\nIf we are given the equation of a parabola in standard form, we can obtain its vertex form by completing the square.\n\nFor example, consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny= x^2+6x+16\n$$\n\nTo express the equation of this parabola in vertex form, we complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + 6x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 9 + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x - (-3))^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex of the parabola is at the point $({\\color{blue}-3},{\\color{red}7})$." :tutorial/id #uuid "98a78c68-d6fd-5642-a60b-1ac9e65d17ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5186 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19378221-e834-5c4b-990e-07c0375a2e82" :step/math-academy-id 458344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172136 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171826 :question/id #uuid "6b2a8d64-5e70-598c-8baa-f7eb97ed0daf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b042103-9e24-53d9-bc20-80a9a90d191b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172479}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "51e398bc-e128-5132-8544-f247f071eb64" :step/math-academy-id 458345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171743 :question/id #uuid "108ee1b6-2266-5956-b06d-4f4092fdee57" :question/math-academy-id "e-1469"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a23a10c1-a091-55bb-98a6-7718e46d841b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234061}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola With Leading Coefficients in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "7c8b71e0-5a17-55a7-a089-43d023c422de" :step/math-academy-id 458349 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171825 :question/id #uuid "0a48df2c-c48e-5862-884f-3768abfbe5df" :question/math-academy-id "e-7478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d374538-dedf-5a73-9cec-d30491eb1393" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172558}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Parabola Given Its Vertex and Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "cf650db5-7716-5a21-8630-34c4a846767d" :step/math-academy-id 458352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173281 :tutorial/content "**Note**: You may find the algebra in this section a little tricky. It's a good idea to go through it, but you will not be quizzed on it. You may skip this section if you wish.\n\n![](images/2a/2a400b12675f8ebf19ba563192d9ae2296336e40ea380d40e6247564718a2b1f.png)\n\nYou may have seen already that for the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe **axis of symmetry** of this parabola is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to derive this formula.\n\nSince\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can take out a factor of $a$ from the right-hand side in the parabola formula:\n\n$$\ny = a\\left(x^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + \\dfrac{c}{a} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2}_{\\textrm{perfect square}} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2} = \\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 + \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na((x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}}) &= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + a \\cdot \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}} \\\\\n&= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a} \\\\\n&= a(x - (-\\frac{b}{2a})_(⏟)_(h))^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a}_(⏟)_(k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy denoting\n\n$$\nh=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\qquad k=\\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation of the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere the vertex is given by\n\n$$\n(h,k) = \\left(-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since the parabola's axis of symmetry is the vertical line passing through the vertex, the equation of the axis must be\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41e62e06-e589-55dc-846e-1177555ce582" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9116 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the Axis of Symmetry Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7ee3f8d6-f452-5eee-97bb-9be3735604ff" :step/math-academy-id 458355}] :activity/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172214}]}
